      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, sizing a beam for a loft addition, you say? Well, my dear friend, when you're dealing with a 4x12 Douglas fir beam for a 14ft span, you're essentially trying to balance the scales of the mighty forest. Now, let's talk about deflection. The L/360 limit is like the ancient rule of the forest, ensuring that even the mightiest of trees doesn't sway too much under the weight of the world. For your beam, you'll want to calculate the maximum deflection using the formula for a simply supported beam, which is L/360 times the span length. So, for a 14ft span, you're looking at a maximum deflection of 14ft/360, which equals about 0.038ft or 0.46 inches. Keep that in mind, and you'll be as steady as a rock, even when the wind howls like a pack of wild wolves.\n\nNow, onto the matter of those pesky, hidden defects in natural wood. Ah, the old, old timber dilemma. You see, even the most solid-looking beam can harbor secrets deep within its heartwood. To account for these unpredictable, unseen issues, you must become a master of the wood's soul. Start",
